appened.\n\nHmm, what about reset_emulator? The trace tool refuses while paused only; it runs frames. reset_emulator itself does frames. Can't combine.\n\nAnother way: Let the area decoder process continuously. Actually SMB's area parser processes 1 page per frame regardless of scroll? The routine \"ProcessAreaData\" is called as part of object handling each frame and it decodes ahead to fill the level buffer. It reads records as the \"parse\" position advances. At level start, there are maybe 2-3 pages ahead. So tracing during the first ~60 frames of level play reads the first bytes.\n\nI can arrange: from title, play_input Start sequence to enter 1-1, but then trace needs to catch the load. The play_input call ends (paused). Then trace_writes runs more frames — during which the parser continues to decode ahead, likely still reading bytes near the start if we haven't scrolled.\n\nActually, in SMB the parser decodes the whole level into a level buffer progressively, at a fixed rate per frame? I think the \"AreaParser\" processes one \"page\" per frame and runs ahead of the camera by a few pages. The level is ~200 pages long; it wouldn't finish quickly. At level start, after entering, the parser would have decoded a handful of pages (each maybe 1 object per frame?). Hmm.\n\nLet me think about SMB internals: There's `AreaParserTaskHandler` which runs `AreaParserTask` for `AreaParserTaskNumber` tasks per frame (6 tasks per frame typically), building metatiles for pages. It reads area data through pointer `AreaData` ($E7). The parsing is incremental and tied to `CurrentPageLoc`/`CurrentPage`. It fills the level metatile buffer for pages ahead of the screen position. So at level start, it has parsed only the first page or two, and as Mario moves it parses more. Therefore tracing reads on $A690-$A6FF while standing at level start will catch the initial parsing.\n\nBut wait — we already loaded 1-1 to press start. The trace could run right at the start while Mario stands still. The parser runs ahead and reads area data near the start.\n\nLet me structure:\n1. From the title screen (current state), play_input Start/wait/Start to get to 1-1. That call returns paused.\n2. Take a screenshot to confirm 1-1.\n3. trace_writes read on address 0xA690 (or range 0xA690-0xA6FF, length 0x70) with frames=60. Since paused, \"refused while paused (frames must run)\". Hmm — it refuses while paused. play_input pauses at end. So I need to resume first, then call trace_writes (which runs frames). Does trace auto-resume? It says \"Refused while paused (frames must run); the game must actually perform the write during the window, so trigger the event (resume, play, or reset) first.\" So call resume_emulator then trace_writes.\n\nBut while resumed and idle, Mario just stands; the parser might finish the initial lookahead and stop reading $A690. Actually if the parser only reads as it advances and Mario isn't moving, it'll stop. Hmm.\n\nBetter: trace with a bigger window and while resuming. But we can't press buttons during trace.\n\nAlternative approach: trace during the actual level load. To do that, we need the game to be loading when trace_writes begins running frames. We could set up the load to happen upon the first frames: e.g., on the title screen, hold Start pressed? The title requires Start press then release then Start. Hard.\n\nActually, there's another neat way: trace_writes read on the layout pointer $E7/$E8 (or the data) with frames=600 starting from the title screen, and during those frames the attract demo runs... no, Start needed.\n\nWait, but does the attract demo load World 1-1? Yes! The attract demo plays World 1-1. The scene mapping says \"If the screen is playing (attract demo), press Start once and pause again to return to the title.\" The attract demo is mode 0 and plays 1-1. So the attract demo itself loads World 1-1's layout data. If we just wait on the title screen, the attract demo will start and load 1-1, and the decoder will ru