{"type":"thinking","text":"Now we have reads. The attract demo loaded World 1-1, and the parser read the records. Key reading PCs:\n- $9516 (LDA ($E7),Y / CMP #$FD) — the check at $9514/$9516, origin prgRom 0x1516. count 72.\n- $9522 (ASL A, after reading second byte) — origin 0x1522.\n- $9533 (AND #$0F) etc.\n- $95A1, $95E9, $95F3, $9600, $962A, $964B — these are in the $95xx region (the object spawner / parser continuation).\n- $9BC0, $9BC7, $9C5C, $9C82 — level-init/pointer code.\n\nThe primary \"reading PC\" for the layout records is $9516 (the CMP after LDA ($E7),Y at $9514) — exactly matching the known parser. Also $9520's read PC is $9522.\n\nSo trace confirms: the area object decoder routine containing $9514 is the reader, and it's active at frames 176-187 (the attract demo's 1-1 load).\n\nNow, \"disassemble back to the routine's entry\": the routine entry is $950A (we found: STX $08 / LDA #0 / STA $0729 / LDY $072c / LDA ($E7),Y...). \n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. The reading PCs include $95A1, $95E9, $95F3, $9600, $962A, $964B. Those are beyond the code I disassembled (which ended around $9588). The routine at $950A might actually extend further and include $9589 ($9589 is INC $072c twice... which is a subroutine) and $9595 (the spawner). Let me look at the code around $9595-$9650 to understand the full routine and whether the entry is $950A.\n\nActually the routine at $950A is likely \"ProcessAreaData\". The subroutines at $9589 and $9595 are called from within it ($956e JSR $9589, $9565 JSR $9595). So $950A is the entry of the routine, and $9589/$9595 are helper subroutines.\n\nBut the reads at $95A1 etc. suggest other code reads the area data too — maybe the object spawner at $9595 reads the object index/attributes. Actually $9595 is the \"spawn object\" routine, and it reads data at $95A1 etc.\n\nHmm wait, the PC $95A1 reading $A691 etc. Actually the trace shows reads addressed $A690, $A691, $A692... with PCs $9516, $9522, $9533, $95A1, $95E9, $95F3, $9600, $962A, $964B, $9BC0, $9BC7. So multiple code sites read the data.\n\nThe task: \"find the routine that reads a level's layout records and builds the level from them (the area object decoder)... get the reading PC, then disassemble back to the routine's entry\". The primary reading PC is $9516 ($9514 + 2), which is inside the routine starting at $950A. But maybe the \"area object decoder\" is a larger routine that starts earlier, e.g., $94E0? No, $94E0 ended with RTS at $9503, and $9504-$9509 is data. So $950A is the entry.\n\nHmm, but let me double-check: is there an entry even earlier, like $94E0 being a different routine (maybe \"AreaParserTask\" that calls the decoder)? The reads at $95A1+ suggest the decoder is a family.\n\nLet me disassemble $9595-$9660 to see the full picture, and also check what calls $950A. Since search for JSR $950A found nothing, maybe it's called via a jump table. Let me search PRG for the two bytes \"0a 95\" and \"14 95\".\n\nAlso, I need to be careful about the \"entry\": The task says record_mapping routine id = entry address, name 'area object decoder'. I should determine the correct entry of the routine that reads the records.\n\nGiven the trace, the reading PC for the records is $9516 (or $9514). The routine's first instruction is $950A. However, is $950A really reached by a JSR? Let me find the caller.\n\nLet me disassemble forward from $950A through $9660, and search for the address in PRG.\n\nAlso, I want to understand the routine enough to write a good note.\n\nLet me do:\n- disassemble $9595 length ~200 (to see the spawner and other readers).\n- search_memory prgRom \"0a 95\" (little-endian pointer to $950A) and \"14 95\".\n- disassemble $950A length 0x160? The tool only printed 80 bytes before. Let me do length 140 from $950A.\n\nWait, earlier disassembling $950A length 80 worked and went to $9556. Let me extend from $9556.\n\nLet me also verify the entry via the caller. Search for \"0a 95\".\n\nLet me run these.\n\nAlso note: the trace should be documented. The routine entry $950A = prgRom offset 0x150A.\n\nNow the proof-by-write: the task requires a ledgered write_memory to prgRom of ONE layout byte, then reset_emulator + screenshot showing the object changed as read by this routine.\n\nFrom the layout data at $A690 (prgRom 0x2690): bytes [07 81 47 24 57 00 63 01 77 01 C9 71 ...]. These are [type, x] pairs. The first object is type $07, x=$81? Hmm, x=$81 = 129 (level x). Actually in SMB area data, each object is [object_id, x_position] where x is in tiles? Hmm. Type $07 = a page-style object? Let me think.\n\nActually the known SMB area object format: The first byte is the object type, the second byte is the x coordinate (in 16-pixel units?). The parser reads type, then x, and builds metatiles.\n\nHmm, actually in SMB the records are [object_type, x] with x in multiples of 16. Wait, from the disassembly:\n$9514: LDA ($E7),Y    ; A = type (first byte)\n$9516: CMP #$FD\n$951a: LDA $0730,X    ; object buffer flags\n...\n$951f: INY\n$9520: LDA ($E7),Y    ; A = second byte (x)\n$9522: ASL A          ; left shift\n$9523: BCC ...\n$9525: ...\n\nSo the second byte is shifted left (x*2?). Then $9531 (after DEX?) reads the first byte again (Y decremented) and AND #$0F, CMP #$0D — checks if type is $0D object (page/screen skip). So the format is indeed [type, x].\n\nFor the proof, I want to change one layout byte so that the built level visibly changes. Good candidates:\n- The first object record's type or x. E.g., change the x byte of an object that places a visible thing near the start.\n\nBut the layout data is read as metatile/objects; changing one byte may produce a visible change or crash. The 1-2 mapping did exactly this: wrote the x byte of the first real object 0x00 -> 0x1F and the first screen rearranged.\n\nFor 1-1, the objects at $A690: [07 81] then [47 24] then [57 00]... Hmm. The first object type $07 with x=$81. Hmm, these look like they might not be [type,x] pairs in the