fts something that's outside the visible window.\n\nHmm, but the trace read offsets 0-11 while building the level. If I change offset 0 to a wildly different type, it should change.\n\nLet me try changing offset 0 from $07 to $0D — type $0D is the special \"page/scroll\" type. Hmm, that might cause weird behavior.\n\nAlternatively change the *sub* byte at offset 2 ($47) — that's likely the actual graphic/object sub-id used to select the metatile! In the spawner, for types<0x0C it reads the third byte and uses bits6-4 as $00 (which indexes the object's graphic). So offset 2's bits 6-4 might select what's drawn. Changing $47 (bits6-4 = 4) to $17 (bits6-4 = 1) would select a different graphic. That could visually change the object!\n\nWait, but $00 gets LSR'd from #$70 mask ($95e9: AND #$70; then $9612 LSR x4). So only bits 6-4 matter at $95e9 path. But there's also $95f3: AND #$0F -> used directly (low nibble). Hmm, different paths.\n\nThis is getting deep. Let me just brute force a couple of candidates:\nCandidate A: offset 0: $07 -> $0D? no.\nCandidate B: offset 2 (0x2692): $47 -> $17.\n\nHmm. Honestly, maybe the simplest reliable visible change: alter the area HEADER? No, that's not the decoder.\n\nNew idea: use a byte that determines a large, obvious feature — the \"level end / flagpole\" or a pipe. But I can't identify it.\n\nAnother idea: scroll right to see more of the level and compare before/after. If I change an object further into the level, I need to travel there. Expensive.\n\nBetter idea: Make the change *and* let the attract demo play (which scrolls through 1-1), then screenshot at various times? The demo is short.\n\nHmm, alternatively: I can write Mario's x to a far position to view a region, but the level buffer is built on the fly... it would build for that region.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: change a byte so that the FIRST SCREEN changes. Let me test offset 0 with several values quickly. Actually, let me test a change that is very likely to