ts \"objects grouped by page in ascending order\" if pages go 0,0,0,7,E?... no, out of order.\n\nHmm, the 1-2 data second bytes: 01,02,00,73,E0,61,62,63,63,62,07,62,E1,52,40. High nibbles: 0,0,0,7,E,6,6,6,6,6,0,6,E,5,4. Not ascending.\n\nHmm. What if the records are [type, x] but the type is the SECOND byte and the first is x? Then [0E 01]: x=0E(14), type=01. [5E 02]: x=5E, type=02. [A7 00]: x=A7, type=00. Hmm.\n\nI think I'm misreading because SMB area data has a special structure: the area data for a level is actually a series of \"pages\", each starting with a byte count or a special marker, and objects are encoded with the x in the high nibble of the first byte etc.\n\nLet me recall the actual SMB format properly. From the SMB disassembly (I've seen this): `AreaData` uses \"objects\" of 2 bytes. The first byte is the object type (0-15 in low nibble) with the high nibble being the y position? No...\n\nActually, I recall now: In SMB, the level's \"object\" data format is:\n- Each object is 2 bytes: byte1 = object type (low nibble) | y-position (high nibble)? \nHmm no.\n\nLet me look at the parser again very carefully:\n$9514: LDA ($E7),Y   ; byte A\n$9516: CMP #$FD\n$9518: BEQ done\n$951a: LDA $0730,X   ; per-object spawn counter\n$951d: BPL ...       ; if bit7 clear, skip (already done?)\n$951f: INY\n$9520: LDA ($E7),Y   ; byte B\n$9522: ASL A         ; bit7 -> carry\n$9523: BCC $9530     ; if bit7 clear skip\n$9525: LDA $072b\n$9528: BNE $9530\n$952a: INC $072b\n$952d: INC $072a     ; page++\n$9530: DEY\n$9531: LDA ($E7),Y   ; byte A again\n$9533: AND #$0F\n$9535: CMP #$0D\n$9537: BNE $9554\n... special type D ...\n\n$9554: CMP #$0E\n$9556: BNE $955d\n... type E ...\n\n$955d: LDA $072a     ; current page\n$9560: CMP $0725     ; target page\n$9563: BCC $956b     ; if page < target, skip (not yet needed)\n$9565: JSR $9595     ; spawn object\n$9568: JMP $9571\n$956b: INC $0729     ; count skipped\n$956e: JSR $9589     ; advance $072c by 2, clear $072b\n$9571: ...loop over objects...\n\nSo $072a is the page counter (incremented by bit7 of byte B). $0725 is the current screen/page being built. Objects on page < target are skipped (INC $0729), objects on page == target are spawned.\n\nHmm OK so bit7 of byte B = page marker (increments page). Then byte B's remaining bits are...? At $9612 the code takes a value from byte A or C to store in $00 (the object's \"y\"?). Hmm.\n\nAnd $072c advances by 2 per processed object ($9589). So records ARE 2 bytes (advance by 2). Then why did I think it reads a third byte? At $95c3+ it does INY and reads ($E7),Y — but that's within the spawner, reading byte B again maybe, not consuming a third record byte. $072c still advances by 2. So records are 2 bytes. Good — my original 2-byte alignment was right!\n\nSo 1-1 records: [07 81][47 24][57 00][63 01][77 01][C9 71][68 F2][E7 73][97 FB][06 83][5C 01][D7 22]...\n\nAnd 1-2 records: [0E 01][5E 02][A7 00][BC 73][1A E0][39 61][58 62][77 63][97 63][B8 62][D6 07][F8 62][19 E1][75 52][86 40]...\n\nNow byte A = type (low nibble) + param (high nibble). Byte B = x (bits 0-6) + page flag (bit7).\n\nPages: 1-2: [0E 01] page0 x1; [5E 02] page0 x2; [A7 00] page0 x0; [BC 73] page0 x0x73=115?? bit7=0 so page0, x=115. Hmm x=115 > 31 (screen width). \n\nHmm, unless x is in some other unit. Or maybe x counts in \"pages\" for some objects. \n\nWait, maybe byte B's bits 6-4 are the page and bits 3-0 the x, and bit7 = \"new page\" flag. For [BC 73]: bits6-4 = 7, x=3. [1A E0]: bits6-4=6, x=0. [39 61]: bits6-4=6, x=1. [58 62]: page6 x2. [77 63]: page6 x3. [97 63]: page6 x3. [B8 62]: page6 x2. [D6 07]: page0 x7. [F8 62]: page6 x2. [19 E1]: page6 x1. [75 52]: page5 x2. [86 40]: page4 x0.\n\nPages: 0,0,0,7,6,6,6,6,6,6,0,6,6,5,4 — still not sorted. Hmm.\n\nBut note the \"new page\" flag (bit7): flags at byte B: 01(no),02(no),00(no),73(no),E0(yes),61(no),62(no),63(no),63(no),62(no),07(no),62(no),E1(yes),52(no),40(no). So only two bit7 flags in the first 15 records of 1-2. And the first three records [0E 01][5E 02][A7 00] have no bit7 flag. Hmm, so page counter starts at 0 and increments at E0. So objects before E0 are page 0.\n\nSo in 1-2, page 0 contains many objects (x up to 0x73?), then page 1 starts at [1A E0]. Hmm.\n\nThen x within page 0 ranges 1,2,0,0x73,... that's weird.\n\nUnless x is in *pixels/16 = tiles* and a page is 256 tiles? No.\n\nOK, radically different idea: maybe byte B is NOT the x; maybe the objects are [y, x] where byte A = y<<4|type? and the x is derived from the parser's current position (each object advances a horizontal cursor by 1 tile), and byte B = additional param. In SMB, I recall the level data is a sequence of *column* commands: for each horizontal position, a list of objects. The parser walks x incrementally!\n\nLook at $9589: INC $072c twice — just advances the data pointer, not x. And $072a (page) increments on bit7 of byte B. Hmm.\n\nActually, I now recall the real SMB format: the area data is a list of objects, and the horizontal position is encoded as the *high nibble of byte A* (the \"y/page\"?) ... no.\n\nLet me look at the spawner more to see how it computes the object's screen position.\n\nHonestly, this is a rabbit hole. Let me take the empirical route: try changing byte B of a record that appears early, and check the screen. I already tried record 0 byte B ($81->$8F) — no change. Let me try record 0 byte A ($07 -> ?) and record 2 byte B, etc.\n\nBut actually — maybe the reason record 0 doesn't matter is that it's on page 0 but x=1 and the visible screen is built from page... hmm.\n\nLet me try a *big* change to record 2's byte B: 0x2695 from $00 to $1F (x 0->31). If that object is in the visible first screen, it'll move.\n\nActually, let me try changing record 4's byte B (0x2699: $01) to something, or record 3 (0x2697: $01).\n\nHmm. I realize I could instead read the *live* parser RAM after loading to see $0725 (target page) and $072a (page), and $072d,X (per-object offsets). That might tell me which objects are on the first screen. But not their screen positions.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: Change a byte and *scroll through the level* to see the effect, using the attract demo (which auto-plays 1-1) or by walking. But comparing \"before vs after\" requires a baseline screenshots at the same positions — doable but expen","truncated":true}
