76-240px = metatile 11-15. No record has x=11-15. So the bush isn't built by these records under this model. Therefore the bush must come from elsewhere → the model is wrong OR the scenery isn't from these.\n\nI think the real SMB format is different: byte A = object type (low nibble) + object-specific param (high nibble), byte B = x position in *screens/tiles* + page flag, where x is in units of 16 pixels but can be large; and objects are NOT grouped strictly by screen — the parser uses the x to place them.\n\nFor 1-1 rec1 [47 24]: x=0x24=36 → 36*16=576px. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively x in pixels/16 but the page flag bit7 adds 256px? rec0 $81 → 256+1=257? rec1 $24=36*16=576...\n\nHmm. If x is in *metatiles* (16px) and each *screen* is 16 metatiles, then x=36 metatiles = screen 2.25. The parser might place objects across screens by their x; the \"page\" is then derived. So records 0-5 build objects at metatile x=1,36,0,1,1,113... no.\n\nUnless x is in pixels/8 = tiles: 1,36,0,1,1,113 tiles → 8px,288px,0,8,8,904px. Still rec1 at 288px.\n\nHmm, 288px and 904px... The first ? block is at 336px. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the FIRST byte's high nibble is the x! rec0: 0, rec1:4, rec2:5, rec3:6, rec4:7, rec5:12. And byte B = y/type. So objects at x-heights...\n\nHmm, byte A high nibbles 0,4,5,6,7,C for records 0-5. Those increase. Could be Y rows (top to bottom). rec0 y0, rec1 y4, rec2 y5, rec3 y6, rec4 y7, rec5 y12. And byte A low nibble = type: 7,7,7,3,7,9. And byte B = x (full, bits0-6): 1,36,0,1,1,113.\n\nHmm y rows 0-12 and x... For the hill (rows 8-12, x 0-5) we'd want records with y 8-12, x small. rec5 y12 x113; no.\n\nFor the bush (rows 9-11, x 22-30 tiles): none.\n\nI'm clearly misparsing.\n\nLet me take a totally different tack: **use trace_writes write mode on the level buffer** to see where these objects get written, or better, use the emulator to correlate. Too deep.\n\nPRAGMATIC DECISION: The task requires a proof that a layout byte is read by the routine. Instead of a pos