{"type":"tool_call","callId":"call_00_TgvgZeHVgfQyrXl4GDEK4804","tool":"record_mapping","input":{"mapping_type":"routine","id":"0x950A","name":"area object decoder","length":127,"confidence":1,"notes":"Area object decoder: reads a level's layout record stream through the pointer $E7/$E8 (set at level start by LoadAreaData $9C22; World 1-1 -> $A690) and builds the level's objects from it. Body $950A-$9588 (RTS at $9588), with helper subroutines at $9589 and $9595. Per record: $950A saves X, clears $0729, loads Y=$072C (byte offset into the stream) and at $9514 does LDA ($E7),Y / CMP #$FD - $FD ends the data. It then reads the record's second byte ($9520, ASL A: bit7 is a page-break flag that increments the page counter $072A/$072B), re-reads the first byte and masks the low nibble for the object type ($9531-$9537, special types $0D/$0E handled at $9539-$9551; otherwise $9554). When the record's page $072A reaches the target page $0725 it calls the object spawner $9595 ($9565); otherwise it counts a skip ($956B) and advances the data offset with $9589 (INC $072C twice + clear $072B). It loops over the up-to-13 object slots ($9571-$957C: LDA/DEC $0730,X) and returns when $0729 and $0728 are clear. HOW FOUND: trace_writes in read mode over the World 1-1 layout data (CPU $A68E-$A78D) while the attract demo loaded 1-1 returned reads from PCs $9516 (LDA ($E7),Y then CMP #$FD), $9522, $9533 and the spawner's $95A1/$95E9/$95F3/$9600/$962A/$964B, all inside this routine. Disassembling backwards from $9516 past the loop head reached the first instruction $950A (preceded by the RTS at $9503 and six data bytes $10 $51 $88 $C0 $A2 $02 at $9504-$9509). Like other SMB routines it has no direct JSR/JMP (searches for '20 0A 95' and '0A 95' found only incidental bytes), so it is reached by the game's indirect/jump-engine dispatch. PROOF (throwaway hack eval-sheet4-b, ledger seq 1): write_memory prgRom 0x2695 (CPU $A695 = the x byte of the layout record [57 00]) 0x00 -> 0x1F, reset_emulator + reapply_edits, re-entered World 1-1; at the identical camera (TIME 390, same two ? blocks and hill on the left) the normal right-side brick/?-block row was replaced by a totally different built structure (a tall green-capped pipe/wall column), i.e. the decoder read the edited record and built a different object. A control write of $FD over the first layout byte (prgRom 0x2690) removed the interactive pipes/? blocks entirely. undo_edit restored 0x00. Combined with the read trace this proves $950A (reading ($E7),Y at $9514) is the routine that builds the level from the layout records."}}
